          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">What is EMDR?</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">EMDR stands for "Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing." Developed by Dr. Francine Shapiro in 1987, the method was originally designed to treat PTSD (Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder).</p>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Today EMDR is recognised by the World Health Organisation (WHO), the American Psychiatric Association (APA) and dozens of international health bodies as a first-line treatment for trauma.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-primary/5 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-primary/15">
            <p class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">WHO and APA endorsed</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">EMDR is one of a very small number of therapy approaches that holds "Grade A evidence" status for trauma treatment — meaning the research supporting it has reached the highest scientific standards.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">How does the brain process trauma?</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">The brain normally digests experiences and stores them in appropriate memory banks. When a traumatic event is too intense or sudden, however, the brain cannot complete this processing.</p>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">As a result, the traumatic memory stays "frozen," locked with its full emotional charge and physical sensations. Even years later it can be recalled with the same overwhelming intensity (flashbacks).</p>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">EMDR "unfreezes" this memory and activates the brain's natural processing mechanism, transforming it into an ordinary memory of the past.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
